  "analysis": "Market read: the last session was a broad risk-off day — nearly every asset closed down (1d returns between -0.2% and -1.7%), while the portfolio sits in a mild -3.15% drawdown with an 'alert' flag (no reduce_only / cap_exposure_60 restriction, but caution is warranted). No news feed is connected, so I rely only on the provided data. The forecast medians are all clustered around zero (consistent with the known lack of directional skill), so I do not chase momentum; instead I use the reliable signal — the width of the 80% interval — plus the drawdown regime. The narrowest 5-day intervals belong to A02 (0.035), A09 bonds (0.040), A06 (0.046), A11 (0.048), A08 (0.051) and A04 (0.052); the widest belong to the single stocks A07 (0.107), A14 (0.093), A01 (0.093), A13 (0.080), A05 (0.077) and the emerging/energy ETFs A12 (0.072), A03 (0.070). Main risks: (1) drawdown continuation after a broad 1d selloff, (2) concentration in the highest-uncertainty single names, (3) over-concentration in one ETF. My proposal keeps a defensive posture — cash 20% (above the 10% floor, below the current 23%) and bonds A09 at 15% (above its 8.85% baseline as a drawdown hedge, trimmed from the current 18%) — and rotates the freed weight into the lowest-uncertainty assets (A02, A06, A04, A11, A08) while cutting the widest-interval, highest-volatility names (A07, A14, A01, A12, A05, A13, A15) toward zero or small residuals. Group caps are respected (us_broad 18%, tech_growth 10%, health 10%, financials 9%, energy 6%, bonds 15%, intl 10%, gold 2%, emerging 0%). All increases above baseline are well within the +15pp asymmetric limit. Confidence is kept modest (0.50-0.58) because my edge is risk-based (interval widths, drawdown regime), not directional.",
